---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import AddPageNumbers from '../islands/AddPageNumbers';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can I skip the cover page?', a: 'Yes. Tick “Don’t number the first page”. Numbering then starts on page 2 with your start number.' },
  { q: 'Can numbering start at a number other than 1?', a: 'Yes. Set <strong>Start at</strong> to any number, which is handy when your PDF continues from another document.' },
  { q: 'Which formats are available?', a: 'Four: <code>1</code>, <code>Page 1</code>, <code>1 / 10</code> and <code>Page 1 of 10</code>.' },
  { q: 'Will numbers appear correctly on landscape or rotated pages?', a: 'Yes. PDFNU places numbers relative to how each page is displayed, so they sit upright in the chosen corner even on rotated pages.' },
  { q: 'Is my document uploaded?', a: 'No. Page numbers are added in your browser and the file never leaves your device.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="add-page-numbers"
  title="Add Page Numbers to PDF Online Free | PDFNU"
  description="Number the pages of a PDF in seconds. Choose the position, format (1, Page 1, 1 of 10) and start number, or skip the cover. Free, no upload."
  h1="Add Page Numbers"
  lead="Number the pages of any PDF. Pick a corner, a format like “Page 1 of 10” and a start number, and skip the cover page if you like. It all runs on your device."
  howToTitle="How to Add Page Numbers"
  steps={['Select or drop your PDF.', 'Choose position, format, start number and size.', 'Click <strong>Add page numbers</strong> and download.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['organize-pdf', 'watermark-pdf', 'merge-pdf', 'split-pdf']}
>
  <AddPageNumbers client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What this tool does</h2>
    <p>
      Add Page Numbers stamps a number on each page of your PDF, in the position and style you choose. It’s useful for theses, reports,
      legal bundles and merged documents, anything people need to reference by page.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open your PDF.</strong> A preview of the first page appears.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose a format:</strong> <code>1</code>, <code>Page 1</code>, <code>1 / 10</code> or <code>Page 1 of 10</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose a position</strong> using the grid: top or bottom, left, center or right.</li>
      <li><strong>Set the start number and size.</strong> Tick the cover option if page 1 shouldn’t be numbered.</li>
      <li><strong>Apply and download.</strong> Your file keeps its name.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Thesis:</strong> skip the cover, start at 1, bottom center, format <code>1</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Court bundle:</strong> top right, <code>Page 1 of 120</code>, size L.</li>
      <li><strong>Second volume of a report:</strong> start at 57 so numbering continues from volume one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Combining several files first? Use <a href="/merge-pdf">Merge PDF</a>, then number the merged document so the count runs straight through.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Numbers use Helvetica in dark gray. If a page already has content in the chosen corner, the number may overlap it, so pick another position.
      Roman numerals aren’t supported yet. Password-protected PDFs must be unlocked first.
    </p>

    <h2>Tips for a professional result</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bottom center</strong> is the most common choice for reports and theses. <strong>Top right</strong> or <strong>bottom right</strong> works well for legal bundles and anything that will be stapled.</li>
      <li>Use <code>Page 1 of 10</code> when pages may be printed and separated. Readers can tell immediately if a page is missing.</li>
      <li>Keep size <strong>M</strong> for normal documents and <strong>L</strong> for slides or scanned pages viewed on phones.</li>
      <li>Numbering a merged file? Merge first, then number, so the sequence runs through all documents.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>Everything happens locally in your browser with pdf-lib. Your PDF isn’t uploaded, logged or kept.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
